Quirks reproduced rather than fixed:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10ms[strlen(s)-1] = '\0'[0m is applied unconditionally, so it strips the newline on a normal line but eats a real data character when the line ended at EOF without a newline or was truncated at the 99-byte [38;5;10mfgets[0m limit ([38;5;10m"abc"[0m with no newline yields 2, not 3).[0m[0m
- The second [38;5;10mfgets[0m uses [38;5;10msizeof(s1)[0m, which happens to equal [38;5;10msizeof(s2)[0m, so it is harmless — left as-is.[0m[0m
- When a line is empty (EOF or a leading NUL byte), [38;5;10mstrlen[0m is 0 and the C writes [38;5;10ms[-1][0m, out of bounds. In this program the byte before either buffer is always zero — [38;5;10mfgets[0m can only ever place a NUL, never data, at index 99 — so the write is unobservable; the Rust skips it and documents why.[0m[0m
- NUL bytes in the input are stored by [38;5;10mfgets[0m but terminate the string for [38;5;10mstrlen[0m/[38;5;10mstrcspn[0m, so [38;5;10m"ab\0cd"[0m behaves as [38;5;10m"a"[0m after truncation. The Rust operates on byte buffers with the same NUL semantics.[0m[0m
